\f1\fs22 Chanta went for a walk.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Across the road, two buses were parked at the shuttle terminus. They had been repurposed as party venues. A guy in a uniform, with an automatic weapon slung over his shoulder, offered Chanta a beer. He accepted. It was ice-cold.\
The crowd in the carpark was sparse at first; clumps of ordinary people wondering about how long they\'92d be locked inside the compound\'97some angry, even while throwing back beer and liquor bottles that must have just grown on Christmas trees. \
Others shrugging, \'93What can you do?\'94\
\'93As long as they keep serving food and drink.\'94\
Chanta found an improvised bar, and trestle tables bearing breads and cold cuts, nuts, fruit, cakes\'85 it seemed Eloise\'92s staff could produce anything in a pinch. The crowd thickened nearer the stage; Chanta pushed his way through.\
A new band started up; they played a familiar tune but Chanta didn\'92t recognise the lyrics. They got through three numbers without a punch-up and seemed like they\'92d make it through their whole set without a single pool cue being cracked.\
None of it gave Chanta any joy. He broke out of the crowd and made for the gates. The same Elvi that had followed him from the shuttle terminus were still tailing him, or at least Elvi of the same clone series descendants. Chanta picked his way around tents and campfires. Underneath his feet the ground was bowling-green flat and uniform with soft grass.\
At the gates the mood was sombre and sour. Chanta picked up words of frustration and anger, and calmer voices explaining conspiracy theories to anyone who would listen. There were some squabbles, but far fewer than Chanta might have expected from a crowd standing like caged zoo animals rather than joining a party.\
He turned and followed the road south, passing between the shuttle terminus and his hover-copt that everyone knew to steer clear of, simply because HARDY INDUSTRIES was emblazoned down both sides in paint that claimed his other hover-copts were even bigger.\
Chanta noticed people peeling out of the carpark towards a block of buildings that could have been offices or a university campus. Chanta chuckled; he\'92d seen the pattern\'97they were wandering off in couples, three or four at the most. There were arms around waists and hands in hands. Chanta knew the proposition in an instant\'97
\f2\i You want to come see where I work?
\f1\i0 \'97as an invitation to find more private spaces for more private embraces.\
Chanta followed, to an open forecourt that ran the length of the first building until it met another wing at right angles. People were heading there, where the wings met to a sign that glowed, 
\fs20 TRAVERSE STATION. 
\fs22 Chanta joined the odyssey down a wide, ramped arcade to an atrium. So did the Elvi tailing him. A bank of elevators loomed. Chanta got into one and was conspicuously the only one not making out. The floor dropped away from Chanta\'92s feet with the acceleration of an amusement park ride. There were wails and laughter; he was surprised no one threw up their alcohol consumption on the half-minute ride. By the time the elevator had slowed to a stop, his knees and his pancreas had never been on closer speaking terms.\
The doors opened into a forecourt. Up ahead there were armed guards checking security passes and looking pass-holders\'92 companions up and down. Chanta drifted to the side to watch, from where he could see dual monorail lines curving into a tunnel. A car appeared out of the tunnel, presented itself sideways to its platform, and opened its doors. The next seven or eight people who had been cleared got in. The pod shut its doors and retraced its path along its rail.\
Sarah was probably down there, at the end somewhere.\
The guards seemed alert; Chanta didn\'92t like his chances of talking his way past them.\
Besides, Sarah might not be down there, even if she\'92d claimed to be in a bunker with Eloise. Chanta didn\'92t know what to think. There were other places he should check, just to make sure. Chanta walked down the road to Sarah\'92s townhouse, followed at a distance by the Elvi who had simply waited for him to return from his amusement park ride.\
Sarah\'92s lights weren\'92t on. He tapped on the front door. He knocked.\
He walked around back and tapped on the sliding glass door. He banged.\
He called Sarah\'92s number. She didn\'92t answer and he could hear no ringtones from anywhere inside.\
All the top floor windows were shut.\
He hoped Alexander wasn\'92t up there, that they were both holding their breaths until he went away.\
He fossicked around front and back doors, looking for where a key might be hidden. That was stupid; if Sarah had hidden a key, it would be in a location that would defeat him.\
He was defeated anyway.
